msgid ""
"</AccordionItem>\n"
"</Accordion>\n"
"In Qiskit, quantum operators are represented using classes from the [`quantum_info`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit/quantum_info) module. The most important operator class is [`SparsePauliOp`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit/qiskit.quantum_info.SparsePauliOp), which represents a general quantum operator as a linear combination of Pauli strings. `SparsePauliOp` is the class most commonly used to represent quantum observables. The rest of this page explains how to use `SparsePauliOp` and other operator classes.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "SparsePauliOp {#sparsepauliop}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The "
"[`SparsePauliOp`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit/qiskit.quantum_info.SparsePauliOp)"
" class represents a linear combination of Pauli strings. There are several "
"ways to initialize a `SparsePauliOp`, but the most flexible way is to use "
"the "
"[`from_sparse_list`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit/qiskit.quantum_info.SparsePauliOp#from_sparse_list)"
" method, as demonstrated in the following code cell. The `from_sparse_list` "
"accepts a list of `(pauli_string, qubit_indices, coefficient)` triplets.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`SparsePauliOp` supports arithmetic operations, as demonstrated in the "
"following code cell.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Pauli {#pauli}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The "
"[`Pauli`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit/qiskit.quantum_info.Pauli)"
" class represents a single Pauli string with an optional phase coefficient "
"from the set $\\set{+1, i, -1, -i}$. A `Pauli` can be initialized by passing"
" a string of characters from the set `{\"I\", \"X\", \"Y\", \"Z\"}`, "
"optionally prefixed by one of `{\"\", \"i\", \"-\", \"-i\"}` to represent "
"the phase coefficient.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The following code cell demonstrates the use of some attributes and "
"methods.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`Pauli` objects possess a number of other methods to manipulate the "
"operators such as determining its adjoint, whether it (anti)commutes with "
"another `Pauli`, and computing the dot product with another `Pauli`. Refer "
"to the [API "
"documentation](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit/qiskit.quantum_info.Pauli)"
" for more info.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Operator {#operator}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The "
"[`Operator`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit/qiskit.quantum_info.Operator)"
" class represents a general linear operator. Unlike `SparsePauliOp`, "
"`Operator` stores the linear operator as a dense matrix. Because the memory "
"required to store a dense matrix scales exponentially with the number of "
"qubits, the `Operator` class is only suitable for use with a small number of"
" qubits.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"You can initialize an `Operator` by directly passing a Numpy array storing "
"the matrix of the operator. For example, the following code cell creates a "
"two-qubit Pauli XX operator:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The operator object stores the underlying matrix, and the input and output "
"dimension of subsystems.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`data`: To access the underlying Numpy array, you can use the "
"`Operator.data` property.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`dims`: To return the total input and output dimension of the operator, you "
"can use the `Operator.dim` property. *Note: the output is returned as a "
"tuple* `(input_dim, output_dim)`, *which is the reverse of the shape of the "
"underlying matrix.*\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The operator class also keeps track of subsystem dimensions, which can be "
"used for composing operators together. These can be accessed using the "
"`input_dims` and `output_dims` functions.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"For $2^N$ by $2^M$ operators, the input and output dimensions are "
"automatically assumed to be M-qubit and N-qubit:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"If the input matrix is not divisible into qubit subsystems, then it will be "
"stored as a single-qubit operator. For example, for a $6\\times6$ matrix:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The input and output dimension can also be manually specified when "
"initializing a new operator:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"You can also extract just the input or output dimensions of a subset of "
"subsystems using the `input_dims` and `output_dims` functions:\n"
msgstr ""

